\subsection{Linked Lists}

\begin{note}
A \emph{linked list} is the simplest of recursive structures.
The \lstinline{Node} class below has an attribute \lstinline{nxt} that points to \lstinline{None} (the base case) or another \lstinline{Node} instance (the recursive case).
The linked lists in this section implement an interface similar to the \lstinline{[]} list type.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
If \lstinline{nxt} is set so that the list points back to an earlier node,
the structure becomes a \emph{circular linked list}.
A circular list still supports \lstinline{__getitem__} for nonnegative indices,
since the recursion still reaches \lstinline{i == 0}.
But \lstinline{__len__} relies on eventually finding \lstinline{nxt is None},
which never happens in a cycle, so it raises \lstinline{RecursionError}.
The same happens to \lstinline{__getitem__} with a negative index,
since \lstinline{i == 0} is never reached.
\end{note}
